\subtree{
  \title{Decision}
  \p{For \code{F : Fun(J,C)}, define \code{Cone(C,F)} using a derived fibre over F of \code{Fun(J^leftcone,C) → Fun(J,C)}, where \code{J^leftcone = [0] ⋆ J} and the vertex is the cone apex. Construct a reduced Segal presentation by choosing its object labels to be the cone data, including the restriction identification with F. In a shape formula using \code{[n] ⋆ J}, fix F and the \em{entire selected cone} at each vertex. A fibre that fixes only the apex objects is insufficient. At \code{[0]} the fibre fixing that cone is a point. Use one functorial replacement/framing for the whole restriction diagram; strict fibres require proved fibrations. Define commas by a specified derived comma construction with its own comparison, not an unnamed join.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Rationale}
  \p{The slice's objects contain maps as well as objects of C.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Rejected}
  \p{Full cone spaces at each chosen cone label; independent fibrant replacement at each simplex.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Acceptance}
  \p{\ref{at-up-0002}: classical cone/slice category; \ref{at-up-0003}: reduction, fibrancy, Segal condition, and categorical invariance; the point/edge calculation in \ref{at-fd-0008}.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Status}
  \p{provisional construction; \ref{ms-0004} export only after these proofs.}
}
